  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The chain, end to end', note: 'Inputs → farm → processing → distribution → retail → table → waste. "Feeding the world" is a system property: production alone has never been the whole answer (enough calories are grown; distribution and waste are the gaps).' },
    { math: '2 · The waste scandal, quantified', note: '⅓ of food produced never gets eaten: post-harvest losses in poor countries (storage, cold chain — the engineering lesson\'s quietest humanitarian mission) and consumer-retail waste in rich ones (the waste lesson\'s food chapter).' },
    { math: '3 · Security = availability + access + stability', note: 'The FAO definition: food must exist, people must afford it, and both must hold through shocks. Famines are almost always access failures (entitlements, Sen) not absolute shortage — the finding that rebuilt famine analysis.' },
    { math: '4 · The 2050 question', note: '~10 billion people, on a warming plateau: the agronomy lesson\'s yield curves must move up while inputs shrink (sustainable intensification); the diet lever (the LCA lesson\'s beef numbers) is the biggest single demand-side option; and the geopolitics of grain trade (Black Sea shocks) made the interdependence legible.' },
  ],
