---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/expert/food-systems-and-security">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Food systems thinking is the agriculture ladder\'s zoom-out: <strong>farms are one stage in a
    chain whose outcomes (security, waste, emissions) are system properties</strong>. The
    discipline measures the whole chain — and finds the leverage points are often not on the
    farm.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the chain, waste, Sen\'s security triad, the 2050 question" widget="equationStep" config={foodSysCfg} />

  <h2>The levers, ranked by evidence</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Yield gaps are the quietest opportunity:</strong> smallholders routinely achieve 30–50% of attainable yield — closing half those gaps (agronomy, roads, storage, credit) out-produces most headline technologies (the development lesson\'s RCT toolkit applies directly).</li>
    <li><strong>Diets shift demand:</strong> global adoption of the EAT-Lancet planetary diet would cut food-system emissions and free land — the behavioural lesson\'s difficulty is the honest footnote.</li>
    <li><strong>Trade buffers shocks and transmits them:</strong> grain reserves and trade kept 2008 and 2022 from being worse — export bans made both worse; the governance lesson\'s collective-action logic, on plates.</li>
    <li><strong>Resilience metrics arrived:</strong> food-system dashboards (reserves, diversity, import dependence) now score countries like climate risk — measurement preceding management, again.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Sen, Poverty and Famines (1981); FAO SOFI reports; EAT-Lancet Commission (2019); the waste and development lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
